<!-- Hand-maintained page; its markdown source of truth is www/docs/xml.md. Keep both in step. -->
<div>
    <!-- Hero Section -->
    <div class="mb-12">
        <nav class="flex items-center gap-2 text-sm mb-4">
            <a href="/docs/builtins" class="text-gray-400 hover:text-white transition-colors">Built-ins</a>
            <svg class="w-4 h-4 text-gray-600" fill="none" viewBox="0 0 24 24" stroke="currentColor">
                <path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M9 5l7 7-7 7" />
            </svg>
            <span class="text-white">XML</span>
        </nav>
        <h1 class="text-4xl font-bold tracking-tight text-white sm:text-5xl mb-6">
            <span class="bg-gradient-to-r from-cyan-400 to-blue-400 bg-clip-text text-transparent">XML</span>
        </h1>
        <p class="text-xl text-gray-300 leading-8 mb-4">Soli parses, queries, builds and streams XML with a built-in Rust parser. A document is a tree of node objects: <code class="text-amber-400">doc.root</code>, <code class="text-amber-400">node["id"]</code>, <code class="text-amber-400">node.text</code>, <code class="text-amber-400">doc.find("channel/title")</code>, <code class="text-amber-400">doc.to_xml</code>. There is nothing to install or import.</p>
        <p class="text-gray-300 mb-4">Four classes are involved:</p>
        <div class="overflow-x-auto mb-6 rounded-xl border border-white/10">
            <table class="w-full text-sm">
                <thead>
                    <tr class="border-b border-white/10">
                        <th class="text-left py-3 px-4 text-gray-400 font-semibold">Class</th>
                        <th class="text-left py-3 px-4 text-gray-400 font-semibold">What it is</th>
                    </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody class="divide-y divide-white/5">
                    <tr>
                        <td class="py-3 px-4 text-gray-300"><code class="text-amber-400">Xml</code></td>
                        <td class="py-3 px-4 text-gray-300">The entry points: <code class="text-amber-400">Xml.parse</code>, <code class="text-amber-400">Xml.parse_file</code>, <code class="text-amber-400">Xml.document</code>, <code class="text-amber-400">Xml.element</code>, <code class="text-amber-400">Xml.build</code>, <code class="text-amber-400">Xml.each</code>, <code class="text-amber-400">Xml.reader</code>, <code class="text-amber-400">Xml.well_formed?</code>, <code class="text-amber-400">Xml.escape</code>. It also holds the XML-signature helpers (<code class="text-amber-400">Xml.c14n_exclusive</code>, …), documented under <a href="/docs/builtins/xml-signatures" class="text-cyan-400 hover:text-cyan-300 underline">XML Signatures</a>.</td>
                    </tr>
                    <tr>
                        <td class="py-3 px-4 text-gray-300"><code class="text-amber-400">XmlNode</code></td>
                        <td class="py-3 px-4 text-gray-300">Every node: elements, text, CDATA, comments, processing instructions. <code class="text-amber-400">node.kind</code> tells them apart.</td>
                    </tr>
                    <tr>
                        <td class="py-3 px-4 text-gray-300"><code class="text-amber-400">XmlDocument</code></td>
                        <td class="py-3 px-4 text-gray-300">The document node, a subclass of <code class="text-amber-400">XmlNode</code>: it has every node method plus <code class="text-amber-400">root</code> and <code class="text-amber-400">declaration</code>.</td>
                    </tr>
                    <tr>
                        <td class="py-3 px-4 text-gray-300"><code class="text-amber-400">XmlReader</code></td>
                        <td class="py-3 px-4 text-gray-300">A pull cursor over a file, from <code class="text-amber-400">Xml.reader</code>.</td>
                    </tr>
                </tbody>
            </table>
        </div>
        <p class="text-gray-300 mb-4">None of them has a constructor: <code class="text-amber-400">XmlNode.new</code>, <code class="text-amber-400">XmlNode.new(...)</code> and <code class="text-amber-400">XmlNode()</code> raise <code class="text-amber-400">XmlNode has no constructor: use Xml.parse, Xml.element or node.add</code>, and the same goes for <code class="text-amber-400">XmlDocument</code> and <code class="text-amber-400">XmlReader</code>.</p>
    </div>

    <section id="quick-start" class="scroll-mt-20 mb-12">
        <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-white mb-4">Quick start</h2>
        <pre data-filename="Example" class="mb-6"><code class="language-soli text-sm">body = """&lt;?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?&gt;
&lt;rss version="2.0"&gt;
  &lt;channel&gt;
    &lt;title&gt;News &amp;amp; views&lt;/title&gt;
    &lt;item id="1"&gt;&lt;title&gt;First&lt;/title&gt;&lt;link&gt;https://example.com/1&lt;/link&gt;&lt;/item&gt;
    &lt;item id="2"&gt;&lt;title&gt;Second&lt;/title&gt;&lt;/item&gt;
  &lt;/channel&gt;
&lt;/rss&gt;"""

doc = Xml.parse(body)
doc.root.name                       # =&gt; "rss"
doc.root["version"]                 # =&gt; "2.0"
doc.find("channel/title").text      # =&gt; "News &amp; views"
doc.find_all("channel/item").each do |item|
  print("#{item["id"]}: #{item.find("title").text}")
end
# 1: First
# 2: Second</code></pre>
    </section>

    <section id="parsing" class="scroll-mt-20 mb-12">
        <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-white mb-4">Parsing</h2>
        <h3 id="xml-parse-xml-options-nil" class="scroll-mt-20 text-xl font-semibold text-white mt-8 mb-3">Xml.parse(xml, options = nil)</h3>
        <p class="text-gray-300 mb-4">Parses a String and returns an <code class="text-amber-400">XmlDocument</code>. It raises on malformed input. <code class="text-amber-400">Xml.parse</code> takes a String only and never treats its argument as a path, so a request body can never turn into a file read.</p>
        <pre data-filename="Example" class="mb-6"><code class="language-soli text-sm">doc = Xml.parse(body)
doc = Xml.parse(body, strip_blank: true)        # drop whitespace-only text nodes
doc = Xml.parse(body, {"max_depth": 32})        # options as one Hash work too</code></pre>
        <div class="overflow-x-auto mb-6 rounded-xl border border-white/10">
            <table class="w-full text-sm">
                <thead>
                    <tr class="border-b border-white/10">
                        <th class="text-left py-3 px-4 text-gray-400 font-semibold">Option</th>
                        <th class="text-left py-3 px-4 text-gray-400 font-semibold">Default</th>
                        <th class="text-left py-3 px-4 text-gray-400 font-semibold">Effect</th>
                    </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody class="divide-y divide-white/5">
                    <tr>
                        <td class="py-3 px-4 text-gray-300"><code class="text-amber-400">strip_blank</code></td>
                        <td class="py-3 px-4 text-gray-300"><code class="text-amber-400">false</code></td>
                        <td class="py-3 px-4 text-gray-300">Drop text nodes that hold only whitespace. Off by default, so the tree is lossless.</td>
                    </tr>
                    <tr>
                        <td class="py-3 px-4 text-gray-300"><code class="text-amber-400">max_depth</code></td>
                        <td class="py-3 px-4 text-gray-300"><code class="text-amber-400">SOLI_MAX_XML_DEPTH</code> (256)</td>
                        <td class="py-3 px-4 text-gray-300">Deepest element nesting accepted.</td>
                    </tr>
                    <tr>
                        <td class="py-3 px-4 text-gray-300"><code class="text-amber-400">max_nodes</code></td>
                        <td class="py-3 px-4 text-gray-300"><code class="text-amber-400">SOLI_MAX_XML_NODES</code> (1,000,000)</td>
                        <td class="py-3 px-4 text-gray-300">Most nodes in the tree.</td>
                    </tr>
                    <tr>
                        <td class="py-3 px-4 text-gray-300"><code class="text-amber-400">max_bytes</code></td>
                        <td class="py-3 px-4 text-gray-300"><code class="text-amber-400">SOLI_MAX_XML_BYTES</code> (64 MiB)</td>
                        <td class="py-3 px-4 text-gray-300">Largest input.</td>
                    </tr>
                </tbody>
            </table>
        </div>
        <p class="text-gray-300 mb-4">The three limits can only be <strong class="text-white">lowered</strong> by an option, never raised: raise the environment variable instead. An unknown option raises <code class="text-amber-400">Xml.parse: unknown option 'deep' (expected one of: strip_blank, max_depth, max_nodes, max_bytes)</code>.</p>
        <p class="text-gray-300 mb-4">What the parser does with the input:</p>
        <ul class="space-y-2 text-gray-300 mb-6 list-disc pl-6">
            <li><strong class="text-white">UTF-8 only.</strong> A byte-order mark is skipped, the <code class="text-amber-400">encoding=</code> declaration is ignored, and invalid UTF-8 raises.</li>
            <li><strong class="text-white">Line endings</strong> are normalised (CRLF and a lone CR become LF) before attribute values are normalised, so a CRLF inside an attribute becomes one space.</li>
            <li>The five predefined entities (<code class="text-amber-400">&amp;amp;</code> <code class="text-amber-400">&amp;lt;</code> <code class="text-amber-400">&amp;gt;</code> <code class="text-amber-400">&amp;quot;</code> <code class="text-amber-400">&amp;apos;</code>) and character references (<code class="text-amber-400">&amp;#233;</code>, <code class="text-amber-400">&amp;#xE9;</code>) are decoded. A reference to a character XML forbids (<code class="text-amber-400">&amp;#0;</code>) raises.</li>
            <li>Text broken up by entity references is joined into one text node. CDATA stays its own node kind.</li>
            <li>It raises on: a <code class="text-amber-400">DOCTYPE</code>, an unknown entity (<code class="text-amber-400">&amp;nbsp;</code>), an unbound namespace prefix, a duplicate attribute, a mismatched or unclosed tag, no root element, a second root element, and non-whitespace text outside the root.</li>
        </ul>
        <h3 id="xml-parse-file-path-options-nil" class="scroll-mt-20 text-xl font-semibold text-white mt-8 mb-3">Xml.parse_file(path, options = nil)</h3>
        <p class="text-gray-300 mb-4">Reads a file and parses it, with the same options. The path goes through the same checks as <code class="text-amber-400">File.read</code>: under <code class="text-amber-400">soli serve</code> and in jobs it must stay inside the application root, a symbolic link is not followed, and a directory, FIFO or device is refused (<code class="text-amber-400">Xml.parse_file: cannot read imports: not a file</code>) without blocking. The file size is checked against <code class="text-amber-400">max_bytes</code> before anything is read.</p>
        <pre data-filename="Example" class="mb-6"><code class="language-soli text-sm">invoice = Xml.parse_file("imports/invoice.xml")</code></pre>
        <h3 id="xml-well-formed-xml" class="scroll-mt-20 text-xl font-semibold text-white mt-8 mb-3">Xml.well_formed?(xml)</h3>
        <p class="text-gray-300 mb-4"><code class="text-amber-400">true</code> if the String parses, <code class="text-amber-400">false</code> otherwise (including for <code class="text-amber-400">nil</code>). It never raises.</p>
        <pre data-filename="Example" class="mb-6"><code class="language-soli text-sm">Xml.well_formed?("&lt;a/&gt;")         # =&gt; true
Xml.well_formed?("&lt;a&gt;&lt;b&gt;&lt;/a&gt;")   # =&gt; false</code></pre>
        <h3 id="xml-escape-text" class="scroll-mt-20 text-xl font-semibold text-white mt-8 mb-3">Xml.escape(text)</h3>
        <p class="text-gray-300 mb-4">Escapes the five XML characters, so the result is safe both in text and inside a quoted attribute value. <code class="text-amber-400">Xml.escape(nil)</code> is <code class="text-amber-400">""</code>. The output is byte for byte the same as <code class="text-amber-400">SOAP.xml_escape</code>.</p>
        <pre data-filename="Example" class="mb-6"><code class="language-soli text-sm">Xml.escape("Tom &amp; \"Jerry\" &lt;3")   # =&gt; "Tom &amp;amp; &amp;quot;Jerry&amp;quot; &amp;lt;3"</code></pre>
        <p class="text-gray-300 mb-4">You rarely need it: the builder escapes everything it writes.</p>
    </section>

    <section id="nodes" class="scroll-mt-20 mb-12">
        <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-white mb-4">Nodes</h2>
        <h3 id="kinds-and-names" class="scroll-mt-20 text-xl font-semibold text-white mt-8 mb-3">Kinds and names</h3>
        <div class="overflow-x-auto mb-6 rounded-xl border border-white/10">
            <table class="w-full text-sm">
                <thead>
                    <tr class="border-b border-white/10">
                        <th class="text-left py-3 px-4 text-gray-400 font-semibold">Member</th>
                        <th class="text-left py-3 px-4 text-gray-400 font-semibold">On an element</th>
                        <th class="text-left py-3 px-4 text-gray-400 font-semibold">On other nodes</th>
                    </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody class="divide-y divide-white/5">
                    <tr>
                        <td class="py-3 px-4 text-gray-300"><code class="text-amber-400">kind</code></td>
                        <td class="py-3 px-4 text-gray-300"><code class="text-amber-400">"element"</code></td>
                        <td class="py-3 px-4 text-gray-300"><code class="text-amber-400">"text"</code>, <code class="text-amber-400">"cdata"</code>, <code class="text-amber-400">"comment"</code>, <code class="text-amber-400">"pi"</code> or <code class="text-amber-400">"document"</code></td>
                    </tr>
                    <tr>
                        <td class="py-3 px-4 text-gray-300"><code class="text-amber-400">element?</code>, <code class="text-amber-400">text?</code>, <code class="text-amber-400">cdata?</code>, <code class="text-amber-400">comment?</code>, <code class="text-amber-400">pi?</code>, <code class="text-amber-400">document?</code></td>
                        <td class="py-3 px-4 text-gray-300">Bool</td>
                        <td class="py-3 px-4 text-gray-300">Bool</td>
                    </tr>
                    <tr>
                        <td class="py-3 px-4 text-gray-300"><code class="text-amber-400">name</code></td>
                        <td class="py-3 px-4 text-gray-300">the qualified name as written: <code class="text-amber-400">"inv:Invoice"</code></td>
                        <td class="py-3 px-4 text-gray-300">a processing instruction's target; otherwise <code class="text-amber-400">nil</code></td>
                    </tr>
                    <tr>
                        <td class="py-3 px-4 text-gray-300"><code class="text-amber-400">local_name</code></td>
                        <td class="py-3 px-4 text-gray-300"><code class="text-amber-400">"Invoice"</code></td>
                        <td class="py-3 px-4 text-gray-300"><code class="text-amber-400">nil</code></td>
                    </tr>
                    <tr>
                        <td class="py-3 px-4 text-gray-300"><code class="text-amber-400">prefix</code></td>
                        <td class="py-3 px-4 text-gray-300"><code class="text-amber-400">"inv"</code>, or <code class="text-amber-400">nil</code></td>
                        <td class="py-3 px-4 text-gray-300"><code class="text-amber-400">nil</code></td>
                    </tr>
                    <tr>
                        <td class="py-3 px-4 text-gray-300"><code class="text-amber-400">namespace</code></td>
                        <td class="py-3 px-4 text-gray-300">the namespace URI, or <code class="text-amber-400">nil</code></td>
                        <td class="py-3 px-4 text-gray-300"><code class="text-amber-400">nil</code></td>
                    </tr>
                    <tr>
                        <td class="py-3 px-4 text-gray-300"><code class="text-amber-400">namespaces</code></td>
                        <td class="py-3 px-4 text-gray-300">Hash of every prefix in scope → URI (<code class="text-amber-400">""</code> is the default namespace)</td>
                        <td class="py-3 px-4 text-gray-300"><code class="text-amber-400">{}</code></td>
                    </tr>
                    <tr>
                        <td class="py-3 px-4 text-gray-300"><code class="text-amber-400">namespace_declarations</code></td>
                        <td class="py-3 px-4 text-gray-300">Hash of the declarations made on this element only</td>
                        <td class="py-3 px-4 text-gray-300"><code class="text-amber-400">{}</code></td>
                    </tr>
                    <tr>
                        <td class="py-3 px-4 text-gray-300"><code class="text-amber-400">path</code></td>
                        <td class="py-3 px-4 text-gray-300"><code class="text-amber-400">"/rss/channel/item[2]"</code></td>
                        <td class="py-3 px-4 text-gray-300">the parent's path plus <code class="text-amber-400">/text()[k]</code></td>
                    </tr>
                </tbody>
            </table>
        </div>
        <p class="text-gray-300 mb-4"><code class="text-amber-400">doc.declaration</code> is the XML declaration as a Hash, or <code class="text-amber-400">nil</code> when the document has none:</p>
        <pre data-filename="Example" class="mb-6"><code class="language-soli text-sm">doc.declaration   # =&gt; {"version": "1.0", "encoding": "UTF-8", "standalone": nil}</code></pre>
        <p class="text-gray-300 mb-4"><code class="text-amber-400">type(node)</code> and <code class="text-amber-400">node.class</code> give <code class="text-amber-400">"XmlNode"</code> or <code class="text-amber-400">"XmlDocument"</code>. <code class="text-amber-400">is_a?</code> takes the class name as a String: <code class="text-amber-400">node.is_a?("XmlNode")</code>.</p>
        <h3 id="navigation" class="scroll-mt-20 text-xl font-semibold text-white mt-8 mb-3">Navigation</h3>
        <p class="text-gray-300 mb-4">Every method that returns an Array returns a fresh one: changing it does not change the tree.</p>
        <div class="overflow-x-auto mb-6 rounded-xl border border-white/10">
            <table class="w-full text-sm">
                <thead>
                    <tr class="border-b border-white/10">
                        <th class="text-left py-3 px-4 text-gray-400 font-semibold">Member</th>
                        <th class="text-left py-3 px-4 text-gray-400 font-semibold">Returns</th>
                    </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody class="divide-y divide-white/5">
                    <tr>
                        <td class="py-3 px-4 text-gray-300"><code class="text-amber-400">doc.root</code></td>
                        <td class="py-3 px-4 text-gray-300">the root element (<code class="text-amber-400">nil</code> only on a document built without one)</td>
                    </tr>
                    <tr>
                        <td class="py-3 px-4 text-gray-300"><code class="text-amber-400">children</code></td>
                        <td class="py-3 px-4 text-gray-300">the <strong class="text-white">element</strong> children, in document order</td>
                    </tr>
                    <tr>
                        <td class="py-3 px-4 text-gray-300"><code class="text-amber-400">nodes</code></td>
                        <td class="py-3 px-4 text-gray-300"><strong class="text-white">every</strong> child node (text, CDATA, comments and PIs included). On a document: what comes before the root, the root, what comes after.</td>
                    </tr>
                    <tr>
                        <td class="py-3 px-4 text-gray-300"><code class="text-amber-400">parent</code></td>
                        <td class="py-3 px-4 text-gray-300">the parent element, or <code class="text-amber-400">nil</code> for the root, a detached node or a streamed match. Reach the document with <code class="text-amber-400">document</code>.</td>
                    </tr>
                    <tr>
                        <td class="py-3 px-4 text-gray-300"><code class="text-amber-400">document</code></td>
                        <td class="py-3 px-4 text-gray-300">the <code class="text-amber-400">XmlDocument</code>, or <code class="text-amber-400">nil</code> for a detached node or a streamed match</td>
                    </tr>
                    <tr>
                        <td class="py-3 px-4 text-gray-300"><code class="text-amber-400">next_sibling</code>, <code class="text-amber-400">previous_sibling</code></td>
                        <td class="py-3 px-4 text-gray-300">the next or previous <strong class="text-white">element</strong> sibling, or <code class="text-amber-400">nil</code></td>
                    </tr>
                    <tr>
                        <td class="py-3 px-4 text-gray-300"><code class="text-amber-400">next_node</code>, <code class="text-amber-400">previous_node</code></td>
                        <td class="py-3 px-4 text-gray-300">the next or previous sibling of any kind, or <code class="text-amber-400">nil</code></td>
                    </tr>
                    <tr>
                        <td class="py-3 px-4 text-gray-300"><code class="text-amber-400">ancestors</code></td>
                        <td class="py-3 px-4 text-gray-300">Array of elements, nearest first</td>
                    </tr>
                    <tr>
                        <td class="py-3 px-4 text-gray-300"><code class="text-amber-400">descendants</code></td>
                        <td class="py-3 px-4 text-gray-300">Array of every element below, in document order</td>
                    </tr>
                </tbody>
            </table>
        </div>
        <pre data-filename="Example" class="mb-6"><code class="language-soli text-sm">channel = doc.root.children[0]
channel.children.map { |child| child.name }   # =&gt; ["title", "item", "item"]
channel.nodes.map { |node| node.kind }        # =&gt; ["text", "element", "text", "element", "text", "element", "text"]
first = channel.children[1]
first.path                                    # =&gt; "/rss/channel/item[1]"
first.next_sibling["id"]                      # =&gt; "2"
first.ancestors.map { |node| node.name }      # =&gt; ["channel", "rss"]
first.document == doc                         # =&gt; true</code></pre>
        <p class="text-gray-300 mb-4">The whitespace between tags is kept as text nodes, which is why <code class="text-amber-400">nodes</code> is longer than <code class="text-amber-400">children</code>. Parse with <code class="text-amber-400">strip_blank: true</code> to drop it.</p>
        <h3 id="text" class="scroll-mt-20 text-xl font-semibold text-white mt-8 mb-3">Text</h3>
        <pre data-filename="Example" class="mb-6"><code class="language-soli text-sm">para = Xml.parse("&lt;p&gt;Hello &lt;b&gt;big&lt;/b&gt; world &amp;amp; more&lt;/p&gt;").root
para.text        # =&gt; "Hello big world &amp; more"       (all the text below, decoded)
para.own_text    # =&gt; "Hello  world &amp; more"          (direct text and CDATA children only)
para.inner_xml   # =&gt; "Hello &lt;b&gt;big&lt;/b&gt; world &amp;amp; more"</code></pre>
        <p class="text-gray-300 mb-4"><code class="text-amber-400">text</code> is the XPath string-value: every descendant text and CDATA node joined, entities decoded, <strong class="text-white">not</strong> trimmed. On a text, CDATA or comment node it is the content; on a processing instruction, its data.</p>
        <h3 id="attributes" class="scroll-mt-20 text-xl font-semibold text-white mt-8 mb-3">Attributes</h3>
        <pre data-filename="Example" class="mb-6"><code class="language-soli text-sm">link = Xml.parse("&lt;a href=\"/x\" xmlns:xl=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink\" xl:title=\"T\"/&gt;").root
link["href"]                                            # =&gt; "/x"
link.get("href")                                        # =&gt; "/x", the same thing
link["missing"]                                         # =&gt; nil
link.attribute("title", "http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink") # =&gt; "T", by namespace URI
link.attributes                                         # =&gt; {"href": "/x", "xl:title": "T"}
link.has_attribute?("href")                             # =&gt; true</code></pre>
        <p class="text-gray-300 mb-4"><code class="text-amber-400">node["name"]</code> looks an attribute up by its name as written (<code class="text-amber-400">"xl:title"</code>). <code class="text-amber-400">attributes</code> keeps source order and leaves out <code class="text-amber-400">xmlns</code> declarations. On a node that is not an element, <code class="text-amber-400">node["x"]</code> is <code class="text-amber-400">nil</code> and <code class="text-amber-400">attributes</code> is <code class="text-amber-400">{}</code>.</p>
    </section>

    <section id="queries-find-and-xpath" class="scroll-mt-20 mb-12">
        <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-white mb-4">Queries: find and xpath</h2>
        <p class="text-gray-300 mb-4">There are two pairs of query methods. They share one grammar and one evaluator, and differ in exactly two ways:</p>
        <div class="overflow-x-auto mb-6 rounded-xl border border-white/10">
            <table class="w-full text-sm">
                <thead>
                    <tr class="border-b border-white/10">
                        <th class="text-left py-3 px-4 text-gray-400 font-semibold"></th>
                        <th class="text-left py-3 px-4 text-gray-400 font-semibold"><code class="text-amber-400">find(expr, ns = nil)</code> / <code class="text-amber-400">find_all(expr, ns = nil)</code></th>
                        <th class="text-left py-3 px-4 text-gray-400 font-semibold"><code class="text-amber-400">at_xpath(expr, ns = nil)</code> / <code class="text-amber-400">xpath(expr, ns = nil)</code></th>
                    </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody class="divide-y divide-white/5">
                    <tr>
                        <td class="py-3 px-4 text-gray-300">Flavour</td>
                        <td class="py-3 px-4 text-gray-300">lenient, like Python's ElementTree</td>
                        <td class="py-3 px-4 text-gray-300">strict XPath 1.0, with Nokogiri's method names</td>
                    </tr>
                    <tr>
                        <td class="py-3 px-4 text-gray-300">A relative path on a <strong class="text-white">document</strong></td>
                        <td class="py-3 px-4 text-gray-300">starts at the <strong class="text-white">root element</strong>: <code class="text-amber-400">doc.find("channel/item")</code></td>
                        <td class="py-3 px-4 text-gray-300">starts at the document node: <code class="text-amber-400">doc.xpath("rss/channel/item")</code></td>
                    </tr>
                    <tr>
                        <td class="py-3 px-4 text-gray-300">A relative path on an element</td>
                        <td class="py-3 px-4 text-gray-300">starts at the element</td>
                        <td class="py-3 px-4 text-gray-300">starts at the element</td>
                    </tr>
                    <tr>
                        <td class="py-3 px-4 text-gray-300">An unprefixed name</td>
                        <td class="py-3 px-4 text-gray-300">matches that local name <strong class="text-white">in any namespace</strong></td>
                        <td class="py-3 px-4 text-gray-300">matches only elements in <strong class="text-white">no namespace</strong></td>
                    </tr>
                    <tr>
                        <td class="py-3 px-4 text-gray-300">A miss</td>
                        <td class="py-3 px-4 text-gray-300"><code class="text-amber-400">find</code> gives <code class="text-amber-400">nil</code>, <code class="text-amber-400">find_all</code> gives <code class="text-amber-400">[]</code></td>
                        <td class="py-3 px-4 text-gray-300"><code class="text-amber-400">at_xpath</code> gives <code class="text-amber-400">nil</code>, <code class="text-amber-400">xpath</code> gives <code class="text-amber-400">[]</code></td>
                    </tr>
                </tbody>
            </table>
        </div>
        <p class="text-gray-300 mb-4">Absolute paths (<code class="text-amber-400">/rss/channel</code>) and <code class="text-amber-400">//name</code> start at the document in both flavours. A consequence of the first row: <code class="text-amber-400">doc.find("rss")</code> is <code class="text-amber-400">nil</code> (it looks for an <code class="text-amber-400">rss</code> child of the root), while <code class="text-amber-400">doc.xpath("rss")</code> finds the root.</p>
        <p class="text-gray-300 mb-4">Results come in document order without duplicates. Elements, comments and other nodes come back as <code class="text-amber-400">XmlNode</code>; a final <code class="text-amber-400">@attr</code> or <code class="text-amber-400">text()</code> step gives Strings:</p>
        <pre data-filename="Example" class="mb-6"><code class="language-soli text-sm">doc.find_all("channel/item/@id")           # =&gt; ["1", "2"]
doc.find("channel/item/title/text()")      # =&gt; "First"
doc.at_xpath("/rss/channel/item[2]")["id"] # =&gt; "2"</code></pre>
        <p class="text-gray-300 mb-4">A node a query returns is the same node navigation reaches: <code class="text-amber-400">==</code> holds between them, and changing one changes the tree.</p>
        <h3 id="namespaces-in-queries" class="scroll-mt-20 text-xl font-semibold text-white mt-8 mb-3">Namespaces in queries</h3>
        <p class="text-gray-300 mb-4">Atom puts every element in a default namespace. <code class="text-amber-400">find</code> reads it without any setup; <code class="text-amber-400">xpath</code> asks you to name the namespace:</p>
        <pre data-filename="Example" class="mb-6"><code class="language-soli text-sm">atom = """&lt;feed xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2005/Atom" xmlns:media="http://search.yahoo.com/mrss/"&gt;
  &lt;title&gt;Blog&lt;/title&gt;
  &lt;entry&gt;&lt;title&gt;One&lt;/title&gt;&lt;link rel="alternate" href="/1"/&gt;&lt;media:thumbnail url="1.png"/&gt;&lt;/entry&gt;
  &lt;entry&gt;&lt;title&gt;Two&lt;/title&gt;&lt;link rel="alternate" href="/2"/&gt;&lt;/entry&gt;
&lt;/feed&gt;"""
feed = Xml.parse(atom)

feed.find_all("entry").length                          # =&gt; 2
feed.find_all("entry/link[@rel='alternate']/@href")    # =&gt; ["/1", "/2"]

feed.xpath("//entry")                                  # =&gt; [] (no namespace)
feed.xpath("//xmlns:entry").length                     # =&gt; 2, the root's default namespace
feed.xpath("//*:entry").length                         # =&gt; 2, any namespace
feed.xpath("//a:entry/a:title/text()", {"a": "http://www.w3.org/2005/Atom"})   # =&gt; ["One", "Two"]
feed.xpath("//entry", {"": "http://www.w3.org/2005/Atom"}).length              # =&gt; 2
feed.at_xpath("//a:entry[2]/a:title/text()", a: "http://www.w3.org/2005/Atom") # =&gt; "Two"</code></pre>
        <ul class="space-y-2 text-gray-300 mb-6 list-disc pl-6">
            <li>A prefix in an expression is resolved, in order, through: the <code class="text-amber-400">ns</code> table passed to the call (a Hash, or named arguments); the namespace declarations <strong class="text-white">on the root element</strong>; <code class="text-amber-400">xmlns:</code>, bound to the root's default namespace; and <code class="text-amber-400">xml</code>, which is always bound. Otherwise the query raises <code class="text-amber-400">Xml.xpath: unbound prefix 'q'</code>. Prefixes are compared by the URI they resolve to, never as text, so any prefix that names the right URI works.</li>
            <li>The <code class="text-amber-400">""</code> key of the table sets the namespace unprefixed names match, in both flavours.</li>
            <li><code class="text-amber-400">*:name</code> matches a local name in any namespace; <code class="text-amber-400">p:*</code> matches every element in one namespace.</li>
            <li>The fallback to the root's declarations only sees the root. A prefix declared lower down, or declared on the root and re-declared lower down with a different URI, needs the table, which always wins.</li>
        </ul>
        <h3 id="the-xpath-subset" class="scroll-mt-20 text-xl font-semibold text-white mt-8 mb-3">The XPath subset</h3>
        <pre data-filename="Grammar" class="mb-6"><code class="language-text text-sm">Expr      := Path ('|' Path)*
Path      := '/' RelPath? | '//' RelPath | RelPath
RelPath   := Step (('/' | '//') Step)*
Step      := '.' | '..' | '@' (QName | '*') | NodeTest Predicate*
NodeTest  := QName | '*' | prefix ':*' | '*:' name
           | 'text()' | 'node()' | 'comment()' | 'processing-instruction(' 'target'? ')'
Predicate := '[' (Integer | 'last()' | Or) ']'
Or        := And ('or' And)*
And       := Test ('and' Test)*
Test      := 'not(' Or ')' | '(' Or ')'
           | Operand (('=' | '!=') Literal)?
           | 'contains(' Operand ',' Literal ')' | 'starts-with(' Operand ',' Literal ')'
           | 'position()' ('=' | '!=' | '&lt;' | '&gt;' | '&lt;=' | '&gt;=') Integer
Operand   := '@' QName | QName | 'text()' | '.'
Literal   := 'single-quoted' | "double-quoted"</code></pre>
        <pre data-filename="Example" class="mb-6"><code class="language-soli text-sm">shop = Xml.parse("&lt;shop&gt;&lt;p sku=\"a\" stock=\"0\"&gt;&lt;n&gt;Lamp&lt;/n&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p sku=\"b\" stock=\"3\"&gt;&lt;n&gt;Desk&lt;/n&gt;&lt;tag&gt;sale&lt;/tag&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p sku=\"c\" stock=\"5\"&gt;&lt;n&gt;Chair&lt;/n&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;/shop&gt;")
shop.find_all("p[@stock!='0']/@sku")                   # =&gt; ["b", "c"]
shop.find_all("p[tag='sale']/n/text()")                # =&gt; ["Desk"]
shop.find("p[2]")["sku"]                               # =&gt; "b"
shop.find("p[last()]")["sku"]                          # =&gt; "c"
shop.find_all("p[position() &gt; 1]/@sku")                # =&gt; ["b", "c"]
shop.find_all("p[not(tag)]/@sku")                      # =&gt; ["a", "c"]
shop.find_all("p[@sku='a' or @sku='c']/n/text()")      # =&gt; ["Lamp", "Chair"]
shop.find_all("p[contains(n, 'ai') and starts-with(@sku, 'c')]/@sku")   # =&gt; ["c"]
shop.xpath("//p/n | //p/tag").map { |node| node.name } # =&gt; ["n", "n", "tag", "n"]</code></pre>
        <ul class="space-y-2 text-gray-300 mb-6 list-disc pl-6">
            <li><code class="text-amber-400">[n]</code> is 1-based and counts per parent, as in XPath: <code class="text-amber-400">//b[1]</code> is the first <code class="text-amber-400">b</code> of <strong class="text-white">each</strong> parent. <code class="text-amber-400">last()</code> is the last of that set.</li>
            <li>Comparisons compare strings, and are true if <strong class="text-white">any</strong> match: <code class="text-amber-400">[tag='x']</code> is true if any <code class="text-amber-400">tag</code> child's text is <code class="text-amber-400">x</code>. <code class="text-amber-400">[@a='v']</code> compares the attribute, <code class="text-amber-400">[text()='x']</code> the direct text runs, <code class="text-amber-400">[.='x']</code> the node's own text. A bare operand (<code class="text-amber-400">[@a]</code>, <code class="text-amber-400">[tag]</code>) tests that it exists.</li>
            <li><code class="text-amber-400">@attr</code> and <code class="text-amber-400">text()</code> may only be the last step of a path.</li>
        </ul>
        <p class="text-gray-300 mb-4">Left out, and refused when the expression is compiled, with the column:</p>
        <ul class="space-y-2 text-gray-300 mb-6 list-disc pl-6">
            <li>axes written <code class="text-amber-400">axis::</code> (<code class="text-amber-400">child::a</code>), variables and arithmetic;</li>
            <li>every function except the ones above (<code class="text-amber-400">Xml.xpath: unsupported XPath at column 1: function 'sum'</code>);</li>
            <li>comparing numbers, except with <code class="text-amber-400">position()</code> (<code class="text-amber-400">//p[@stock &gt; 1]</code>);</li>
            <li>a path inside a predicate: an operand is one child step or attribute (<code class="text-amber-400">//p[n/text()='Lamp']</code>; write <code class="text-amber-400">//p[n='Lamp']</code>);</li>
            <li>a parenthesised path: for <code class="text-amber-400">(//a)[1]</code> write <code class="text-amber-400">at_xpath("//a")</code>.</li>
        </ul>
        <p class="text-gray-300 mb-4">There are two kinds of error, <code class="text-amber-400">invalid XPath</code> (a syntax error) and <code class="text-amber-400">unsupported XPath</code> (valid XPath this subset leaves out). <code class="text-amber-400">find</code> and <code class="text-amber-400">find_all</code> errors start with <code class="text-amber-400">Xml.find:</code>, the others with <code class="text-amber-400">Xml.xpath:</code>.</p>
        <p class="text-gray-300 mb-4">A <strong class="text-white">literal</strong> expression is compiled when the file is checked, so <code class="text-amber-400">soli check</code> (and the check <code class="text-amber-400">soli</code> runs before a script) refuses <code class="text-amber-400">doc.xpath("sum(//p)")</code> before the program starts. An expression built at run time is compiled when it runs.</p>
        <h3 id="query-limits" class="scroll-mt-20 text-xl font-semibold text-white mt-8 mb-3">Query limits</h3>
        <ul class="space-y-2 text-gray-300 mb-6 list-disc pl-6">
            <li>An expression is at most 4096 bytes, 64 steps, 8 predicates per step, 16 <code class="text-amber-400">and</code>/<code class="text-amber-400">or</code> terms per predicate, and 16 levels of <code class="text-amber-400">not(…)</code> and parentheses. Past one: <code class="text-amber-400">XPath too complex: more than 64 steps</code>.</li>
            <li>One evaluation visits at most <code class="text-amber-400">SOLI_MAX_XPATH_VISITS</code> nodes (10,000,000 by default), plus one visit per 64 bytes of text it copies to compare. Past it: <code class="text-amber-400">Xml.xpath: evaluation exceeded 10000000 node visits (SOLI_MAX_XPATH_VISITS)</code>. A step visits each node at most once, so an ordinary query costs at most steps × nodes.</li>
            <li>Compiled expressions are cached (128 per thread), so a query in a loop is compiled once.</li>
        </ul>
    </section>

    <section id="building" class="scroll-mt-20 mb-12">
        <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-white mb-4">Building</h2>
        <h3 id="xml-document-xml-element-and-node-add" class="scroll-mt-20 text-xl font-semibold text-white mt-8 mb-3">Xml.document, Xml.element and node.add</h3>
        <pre data-filename="Example" class="mb-6"><code class="language-soli text-sm">posts = [
  {"id": 1, "title": "XML lands", "url": "https://example.com/1", "body_html": "&lt;p&gt;Hi&lt;/p&gt;"},
  {"id": 2, "title": "Tom &amp; Jerry", "url": "https://example.com/2", "body_html": "&lt;p&gt;Yo&lt;/p&gt;"}
]

doc = Xml.document("rss", version: "2.0")
channel = doc.root.add("channel")
channel.add("title", "News &amp; views")
posts.each do |post|
  item = channel.add("item", id: post.id)
  item.add("title", post.title)
  item.add("link", post.url, rel: "alternate")
  item.add_cdata(post.body_html)
end
print(doc.to_xml(indent: 2))</code></pre>
        <pre data-filename="Output" class="mb-6"><code class="language-xml text-sm">&lt;?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?&gt;
&lt;rss version="2.0"&gt;
  &lt;channel&gt;
    &lt;title&gt;News &amp;amp; views&lt;/title&gt;
    &lt;item id="1"&gt;&lt;title&gt;XML lands&lt;/title&gt;&lt;link rel="alternate"&gt;https://example.com/1&lt;/link&gt;&lt;![CDATA[&lt;p&gt;Hi&lt;/p&gt;]]&gt;&lt;/item&gt;
    &lt;item id="2"&gt;&lt;title&gt;Tom &amp;amp; Jerry&lt;/title&gt;&lt;link rel="alternate"&gt;https://example.com/2&lt;/link&gt;&lt;![CDATA[&lt;p&gt;Yo&lt;/p&gt;]]&gt;&lt;/item&gt;
  &lt;/channel&gt;
&lt;/rss&gt;</code></pre>
        <p class="text-gray-300 mb-4">(<code class="text-amber-400">indent</code> only indents elements whose content is all elements, so the mixed <code class="text-amber-400">item</code>s stay on one line.)</p>
        <div class="overflow-x-auto mb-6 rounded-xl border border-white/10">
            <table class="w-full text-sm">
                <thead>
                    <tr class="border-b border-white/10">
                        <th class="text-left py-3 px-4 text-gray-400 font-semibold">Call</th>
                        <th class="text-left py-3 px-4 text-gray-400 font-semibold">Returns</th>
                    </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody class="divide-y divide-white/5">
                    <tr>
                        <td class="py-3 px-4 text-gray-300"><code class="text-amber-400">Xml.document(root_name = nil, attrs = nil)</code></td>
                        <td class="py-3 px-4 text-gray-300">a new <code class="text-amber-400">XmlDocument</code>, with a root element when <code class="text-amber-400">root_name</code> is given. It writes an XML declaration by default.</td>
                    </tr>
                    <tr>
                        <td class="py-3 px-4 text-gray-300"><code class="text-amber-400">Xml.element(name, content = nil, attrs = nil)</code></td>
                        <td class="py-3 px-4 text-gray-300">a detached element in a tree of its own</td>
                    </tr>
                    <tr>
                        <td class="py-3 px-4 text-gray-300"><code class="text-amber-400">node.add(name, content = nil, attrs = nil)</code></td>
                        <td class="py-3 px-4 text-gray-300">appends a new element child to <code class="text-amber-400">node</code> and returns the <strong class="text-white">child</strong></td>
                    </tr>
                </tbody>
            </table>
        </div>
        <p class="text-gray-300 mb-4"><code class="text-amber-400">content</code> and <code class="text-amber-400">attrs</code> follow the same rules everywhere:</p>
        <ul class="space-y-2 text-gray-300 mb-6 list-disc pl-6">
            <li><code class="text-amber-400">content</code> is a String, Int, Float, Bool or Decimal (written with <code class="text-amber-400">.to_s</code>), an <code class="text-amber-400">XmlNode</code>, or an Array of these.</li>
            <li>A Hash in the content position is taken as the attributes, so <code class="text-amber-400">add("item", id: 1)</code> and <code class="text-amber-400">add("item", {"id": 1})</code> both work. Named arguments are attributes.</li>
            <li>An attribute whose value is <code class="text-amber-400">nil</code> is left out.</li>
            <li>An <code class="text-amber-400">xmlns</code> or <code class="text-amber-400">xmlns:p</code> key declares a namespace.</li>
            <li>A prefixed name (<code class="text-amber-400">inv:Line</code>) must be bound by a declaration in scope when the node is created, or the call raises <code class="text-amber-400">XmlNode.add: unbound namespace prefix 'cbc'</code>.</li>
        </ul>
        <pre data-filename="Example" class="mb-6"><code class="language-soli text-sm">cell = Xml.element("td", ["Total: ", Xml.element("b", 42)], {"class": "sum"})
cell.to_xml                                   # =&gt; "&lt;td class=\"sum\"&gt;Total: &lt;b&gt;42&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/td&gt;"
Xml.element("x", {"k": "v", "skip": nil}).to_xml   # =&gt; "&lt;x k=\"v\"/&gt;"

invoice = Xml.document("inv:Invoice", {"xmlns:inv": "urn:inv"})
invoice.root.add("inv:Line", "1").namespace   # =&gt; "urn:inv"</code></pre>
        <p class="text-gray-300 mb-4"><code class="text-amber-400">add</code> is all-or-nothing: if its content fails (for example a node that would become its own ancestor), no element is left behind.</p>
        <h3 id="blocks-xml-build-and-node-add-do" class="scroll-mt-20 text-xl font-semibold text-white mt-8 mb-3">Blocks: Xml.build and node.add … do</h3>
        <p class="text-gray-300 mb-4"><code class="text-amber-400">Xml.build(root_name, attrs = nil) do |root| … end</code> creates a document, runs the block with its root element, and returns the document (with a declaration). <code class="text-amber-400">node.add(name, content = nil, attrs = nil) do |child| … end</code> appends the child, runs the block with it, and returns the child.</p>
        <pre data-filename="Example" class="mb-6"><code class="language-soli text-sm">feed = Xml.build("feed", {"xmlns": "http://www.w3.org/2005/Atom"}) do |root|
  root.add("title", "Blog")
  posts.each do |post|
    root.add("entry") do |entry|
      entry.add("title", post.title)
      entry.add("link", nil, href: post.url)
    end
  end
end
print(feed.to_xml(indent: 2))</code></pre>
        <pre data-filename="Output" class="mb-6"><code class="language-xml text-sm">&lt;?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?&gt;
&lt;feed xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2005/Atom"&gt;
  &lt;title&gt;Blog&lt;/title&gt;
  &lt;entry&gt;
    &lt;title&gt;XML lands&lt;/title&gt;
    &lt;link href="https://example.com/1"/&gt;
  &lt;/entry&gt;
  &lt;entry&gt;
    &lt;title&gt;Tom &amp;amp; Jerry&lt;/title&gt;
    &lt;link href="https://example.com/2"/&gt;
  &lt;/entry&gt;
&lt;/feed&gt;</code></pre>
        <ul class="space-y-2 text-gray-300 mb-6 list-disc pl-6">
            <li>The block needs a parameter (<code class="text-amber-400">|entry|</code>). A lambda works too: <code class="text-amber-400">root.add("a", &amp;builder)</code>, or passed as the last argument.</li>
            <li>The block sees and updates the variables around it. An error raised inside it propagates. The request deadline is checked before each block runs.</li>
            <li><code class="text-amber-400">return</code>, <code class="text-amber-400">break</code> and <code class="text-amber-400">next</code> inside a builder block only leave that block.</li>
            <li><code class="text-amber-400">&amp;.</code> works with named arguments and blocks, and evaluates neither on <code class="text-amber-400">nil</code>: <code class="text-amber-400">parent&amp;.add("x", id: 1) do |x| … end</code>.</li>
        </ul>
        <h3 id="other-kinds-of-node" class="scroll-mt-20 text-xl font-semibold text-white mt-8 mb-3">Other kinds of node</h3>
        <pre data-filename="Example" class="mb-6"><code class="language-soli text-sm">root = Xml.document("r").root
root.add_text("a &lt; b")
root.add_comment("generated")
root.add_pi("xml-stylesheet", "href=\"style.xsl\"")
root.to_xml   # =&gt; "&lt;r&gt;a &amp;lt; b&lt;!--generated--&gt;&lt;?xml-stylesheet href=\"style.xsl\"?&gt;&lt;/r&gt;"</code></pre>
        <p class="text-gray-300 mb-4"><code class="text-amber-400">add_text(s)</code>, <code class="text-amber-400">add_cdata(s)</code>, <code class="text-amber-400">add_comment(s)</code> and <code class="text-amber-400">add_pi(target, data = "")</code> each append one node and return it. A CDATA section containing <code class="text-amber-400">]]&gt;</code> is split into two sections when written.</p>
        <h3 id="validation" class="scroll-mt-20 text-xl font-semibold text-white mt-8 mb-3">Validation</h3>
        <ul class="space-y-2 text-gray-300 mb-6 list-disc pl-6">
            <li>Names are checked as XML qualified names: <code class="text-amber-400">Xml.element("1bad")</code> raises <code class="text-amber-400">Xml.element: invalid XML name '1bad'</code>.</li>
            <li>Characters XML forbids in text and attribute values (the C0 controls other than tab, LF and CR, plus U+FFFE and U+FFFF) are <strong class="text-white">dropped</strong>, so a stray control character in a database row cannot make the output unreadable: <code class="text-amber-400">Xml.element("t", "a\u0001b").to_xml</code> is <code class="text-amber-400">"&lt;t&gt;ab&lt;/t&gt;"</code>.</li>
            <li>A comment containing <code class="text-amber-400">--</code> or ending with <code class="text-amber-400">-</code> raises, and so does a processing instruction whose target is <code class="text-amber-400">xml</code>.</li>
            <li>One element holds at most <code class="text-amber-400">SOLI_MAX_XML_ATTRS</code> attributes, and a tree at most <code class="text-amber-400">SOLI_MAX_XML_NODES</code> nodes.</li>
        </ul>
    </section>

    <section id="changing-a-tree" class="scroll-mt-20 mb-12">
        <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-white mb-4">Changing a tree</h2>
        <pre data-filename="Example" class="mb-6"><code class="language-soli text-sm">list = Xml.parse("&lt;list&gt;&lt;item id=\"1\"&gt;old&lt;/item&gt;&lt;item id=\"2\"/&gt;&lt;draft/&gt;&lt;/list&gt;")
entry = list.find("item")
entry["id"] = 10                  # set an attribute (written with .to_s)
entry["lang"] = "en"
entry.text = "new &amp; shiny"        # replace every child with one text node
entry.name = "entry"              # rename (the name is validated)
list.root.to_xml   # =&gt; "&lt;list&gt;&lt;entry id=\"10\" lang=\"en\"&gt;new &amp;amp; shiny&lt;/entry&gt;&lt;item id=\"2\"/&gt;&lt;draft/&gt;&lt;/list&gt;"

entry.remove_attribute("lang")    # =&gt; "en" (nil when it was not there)
entry["id"] = nil                 # nil removes an attribute

draft = list.find("draft").remove # detach; the node stays usable
second = list.find("item[@id='2']")
second.insert_before(Xml.element("sep"))
second.insert_after("tail text")  # a String becomes a text node
list.root.prepend(Xml.element("head"))
list.root.append(draft)
second.replace(Xml.element("swapped"))
list.root.to_xml   # =&gt; "&lt;list&gt;&lt;head/&gt;&lt;entry&gt;new &amp;amp; shiny&lt;/entry&gt;&lt;sep/&gt;&lt;swapped/&gt;tail text&lt;draft/&gt;&lt;/list&gt;"</code></pre>
        <div class="overflow-x-auto mb-6 rounded-xl border border-white/10">
            <table class="w-full text-sm">
                <thead>
                    <tr class="border-b border-white/10">
                        <th class="text-left py-3 px-4 text-gray-400 font-semibold">Member</th>
                        <th class="text-left py-3 px-4 text-gray-400 font-semibold">Does</th>
                    </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody class="divide-y divide-white/5">
                    <tr>
                        <td class="py-3 px-4 text-gray-300"><code class="text-amber-400">node["name"] = value</code>, <code class="text-amber-400">node.set("name", value)</code></td>
                        <td class="py-3 px-4 text-gray-300">set an attribute (String, Int, Float, Bool or Decimal); <code class="text-amber-400">nil</code> removes it</td>
                    </tr>
                    <tr>
                        <td class="py-3 px-4 text-gray-300"><code class="text-amber-400">remove_attribute(name)</code></td>
                        <td class="py-3 px-4 text-gray-300">remove it and return its value, or <code class="text-amber-400">nil</code></td>
                    </tr>
                    <tr>
                        <td class="py-3 px-4 text-gray-300"><code class="text-amber-400">node.text = s</code></td>
                        <td class="py-3 px-4 text-gray-300">replace all children with one text node (refused on a document)</td>
                    </tr>
                    <tr>
                        <td class="py-3 px-4 text-gray-300"><code class="text-amber-400">node.name = s</code></td>
                        <td class="py-3 px-4 text-gray-300">rename, after checking the name</td>
                    </tr>
                    <tr>
                        <td class="py-3 px-4 text-gray-300"><code class="text-amber-400">append(x)</code>, <code class="text-amber-400">prepend(x)</code></td>
                        <td class="py-3 px-4 text-gray-300">add <code class="text-amber-400">x</code> (an <code class="text-amber-400">XmlNode</code>, or a String for a text node) as the last or first child</td>
                    </tr>
                    <tr>
                        <td class="py-3 px-4 text-gray-300"><code class="text-amber-400">insert_before(x)</code>, <code class="text-amber-400">insert_after(x)</code>, <code class="text-amber-400">replace(x)</code></td>
                        <td class="py-3 px-4 text-gray-300">the same, as a sibling</td>
                    </tr>
                    <tr>
                        <td class="py-3 px-4 text-gray-300"><code class="text-amber-400">remove</code></td>
                        <td class="py-3 px-4 text-gray-300">detach the node and return it: it becomes the root of its own fragment</td>
                    </tr>
                    <tr>
                        <td class="py-3 px-4 text-gray-300"><code class="text-amber-400">dup</code></td>
                        <td class="py-3 px-4 text-gray-300">a deep copy in a fresh tree</td>
                    </tr>
                </tbody>
            </table>
        </div>
        <p class="text-gray-300 mb-4">These return the node they inserted or affected.</p>
        <ul class="space-y-2 text-gray-300 mb-6 list-disc pl-6">
            <li>A node from the <strong class="text-white">same</strong> tree is <strong class="text-white">moved</strong>. Moving a node into itself or into one of its descendants raises <code class="text-amber-400">would create a cycle</code>.</li>
            <li>A node from <strong class="text-white">another</strong> tree is <strong class="text-white">copied</strong>, and the copy is returned: the original stays where it was.</li>
            <li><code class="text-amber-400">node.text=</code> and <code class="text-amber-400">node.name=</code> are the only writable properties. Any other dot write (<code class="text-amber-400">node.foo = 1</code>) raises <code class="text-amber-400">XmlNode has no writable property 'foo'</code>, and <code class="text-amber-400">soli check</code> refuses it too.</li>
            <li>A handle to any node keeps its whole tree alive, and a removed node stays in its tree (counted by <code class="text-amber-400">SOLI_MAX_XML_NODES</code>) until the tree is freed. To keep a small part of a large document, <code class="text-amber-400">dup</code> it: the copy is a compact tree of its own.</li>
            <li>A parsed <code class="text-amber-400">&lt;a&gt;&lt;/a&gt;</code> keeps its expanded form even after its children move out.</li>
        </ul>
    </section>

    <section id="serialisation" class="scroll-mt-20 mb-12">
        <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-white mb-4">Serialisation</h2>
        <p class="text-gray-300 mb-4"><code class="text-amber-400">node.to_xml(options = nil)</code>, and <code class="text-amber-400">to_s</code>, which is the same thing.</p>
        <div class="overflow-x-auto mb-6 rounded-xl border border-white/10">
            <table class="w-full text-sm">
                <thead>
                    <tr class="border-b border-white/10">
                        <th class="text-left py-3 px-4 text-gray-400 font-semibold">Option</th>
                        <th class="text-left py-3 px-4 text-gray-400 font-semibold">Default</th>
                        <th class="text-left py-3 px-4 text-gray-400 font-semibold">Effect</th>
                    </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody class="divide-y divide-white/5">
                    <tr>
                        <td class="py-3 px-4 text-gray-300"><code class="text-amber-400">indent</code></td>
                        <td class="py-3 px-4 text-gray-300"><code class="text-amber-400">nil</code> (compact)</td>
                        <td class="py-3 px-4 text-gray-300">An Int (above 64 counts as 64) indents elements whose content is all elements. Mixed content is never touched.</td>
                    </tr>
                    <tr>
                        <td class="py-3 px-4 text-gray-300"><code class="text-amber-400">declaration</code></td>
                        <td class="py-3 px-4 text-gray-300">on a document: <code class="text-amber-400">true</code> if it was parsed with one or made by <code class="text-amber-400">Xml.document</code> / <code class="text-amber-400">Xml.build</code>; on a node: <code class="text-amber-400">false</code></td>
                        <td class="py-3 px-4 text-gray-300">Write <code class="text-amber-400">&lt;?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?&gt;</code>.</td>
                    </tr>
                    <tr>
                        <td class="py-3 px-4 text-gray-300"><code class="text-amber-400">expand_empty</code></td>
                        <td class="py-3 px-4 text-gray-300"><code class="text-amber-400">false</code></td>
                        <td class="py-3 px-4 text-gray-300">Write <code class="text-amber-400">&lt;a&gt;&lt;/a&gt;</code> instead of <code class="text-amber-400">&lt;a/&gt;</code>.</td>
                    </tr>
                </tbody>
            </table>
        </div>
        <pre data-filename="Example" class="mb-6"><code class="language-soli text-sm">source = "&lt;a x=\"1\"&gt;&lt;b/&gt;&lt;c&gt;&lt;/c&gt;&lt;!--k--&gt;&lt;?p d?&gt;&lt;/a&gt;"
Xml.parse(source).to_xml == source                  # =&gt; true
Xml.parse("&lt;a/&gt;").to_xml(expand_empty: true)        # =&gt; "&lt;a&gt;&lt;/a&gt;"
Xml.document("r").to_xml(declaration: false)        # =&gt; "&lt;r/&gt;"
Xml.parse("&lt;r xmlns:p=\"urn:p\"&gt;&lt;p:a/&gt;&lt;/r&gt;").root.children[0].to_xml   # =&gt; "&lt;p:a xmlns:p=\"urn:p\"/&gt;"</code></pre>
        <ul class="space-y-2 text-gray-300 mb-6 list-disc pl-6">
            <li>With no options the output is <strong class="text-white">lossless</strong>: an element keeps the self-closing form it was parsed with, attributes and namespace declarations keep their order and prefixes, and values are always written between <code class="text-amber-400">"</code>. Built empty elements are written <code class="text-amber-400">&lt;a/&gt;</code>.</li>
            <li>An element's <code class="text-amber-400">to_xml</code> is a well-formed fragment on its own: the namespace declarations it inherits and uses are written on its start tag.</li>
            <li>Escaping: text escapes <code class="text-amber-400">&amp; &lt; &gt;</code> (and CR as <code class="text-amber-400">&amp;#13;</code>); attribute values escape <code class="text-amber-400">&amp; &lt; &gt; "</code> plus tab, LF and CR as character references, so they survive a re-parse.</li>
            <li>The output is capped at <code class="text-amber-400">SOLI_MAX_XML_BYTES</code>.</li>
        </ul>
        <p class="text-gray-300 mb-4"><code class="text-amber-400">print(node)</code>, <code class="text-amber-400">"#{node}"</code> and <code class="text-amber-400">node.inspect</code> all show the compact <code class="text-amber-400">to_xml</code>. <code class="text-amber-400">json_stringify</code> and <code class="text-amber-400">render_json</code> write a node as its XML string, as they do for a <code class="text-amber-400">DateTime</code>: use <code class="text-amber-400">to_h</code> for structured JSON.</p>
    </section>

    <section id="to-h" class="scroll-mt-20 mb-12">
        <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-white mb-4">to_h</h2>
        <p class="text-gray-300 mb-4"><code class="text-amber-400">to_h</code> is a lossy but convenient Hash view, handy with <code class="text-amber-400">permit</code> or <code class="text-amber-400">render_json</code>:</p>
        <pre data-filename="Example" class="mb-6"><code class="language-soli text-sm">Xml.parse("&lt;item id=\"1\"&gt;&lt;title&gt;A &amp;amp; B&lt;/title&gt;&lt;tag&gt;x&lt;/tag&gt;&lt;tag&gt;y&lt;/tag&gt;&lt;empty/&gt;&lt;/item&gt;").to_h
# =&gt; {"item": {"@id": "1", "title": "A &amp; B", "tag": ["x", "y"], "empty": nil}}

Xml.parse("&lt;a x=\"1\"&gt;t&lt;b/&gt;&lt;/a&gt;").to_h
# =&gt; {"a": {"@x": "1", "b": nil, "_text": "t"}}

row = Xml.parse("&lt;product sku=\"a1\"&gt;&lt;name&gt;Lamp&lt;/name&gt;&lt;price&gt;9.5&lt;/price&gt;&lt;/product&gt;").to_h["product"]
permit(row, {"name": true, "price": true})   # =&gt; {"name": "Lamp", "price": "9.5"}</code></pre>
        <ul class="space-y-2 text-gray-300 mb-6 list-disc pl-6">
            <li><code class="text-amber-400">element.to_h</code> is <code class="text-amber-400">{name =&gt; value}</code>; <code class="text-amber-400">doc.to_h</code> is <code class="text-amber-400">doc.root.to_h</code>.</li>
            <li>An element with no attributes and no element children is its text, trimmed, or <code class="text-amber-400">nil</code> when empty.</li>
            <li>Otherwise its value is a Hash: attributes under <code class="text-amber-400">"@name"</code>, children by name (a repeated name becomes an Array), and non-blank text under <code class="text-amber-400">"_text"</code>.</li>
            <li>Every value is a String: <code class="text-amber-400">"9.5"</code> stays a String.</li>
            <li>Nesting deeper than 1024 levels is refused.</li>
        </ul>
        <p class="text-gray-300 mb-4"><code class="text-amber-400">to_h</code> differs from <code class="text-amber-400">SOAP.parse</code>, which drops attributes. See <a href="/docs/builtins/soap" class="text-cyan-400 hover:text-cyan-300 underline">SOAP</a>.</p>
    </section>

    <section id="streaming-large-files" class="scroll-mt-20 mb-12">
        <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-white mb-4">Streaming large files</h2>
        <p class="text-gray-300 mb-4"><code class="text-amber-400">Xml.each</code> and <code class="text-amber-400">Xml.reader</code> read a file one element at a time, so a file of any size is handled in a bounded amount of memory (200 MiB streamed with under 2 MiB of extra memory).</p>
        <pre data-filename="Example" class="mb-6"><code class="language-soli text-sm">total = Xml.each("exports/catalog.xml", "product[@status='active']") do |product|
  print(product.find("name").text)
end
# total is the number of matches

reader = Xml.reader("exports/catalog.xml", "product")
found = nil
while (product = reader.next)
  if product["sku"] == "b2"
    found = product
    break
  end
end
reader.close   # optional: the file is closed when the reader is dropped</code></pre>
        <div class="overflow-x-auto mb-6 rounded-xl border border-white/10">
            <table class="w-full text-sm">
                <thead>
                    <tr class="border-b border-white/10">
                        <th class="text-left py-3 px-4 text-gray-400 font-semibold">Call</th>
                        <th class="text-left py-3 px-4 text-gray-400 font-semibold">Returns</th>
                    </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody class="divide-y divide-white/5">
                    <tr>
                        <td class="py-3 px-4 text-gray-300"><code class="text-amber-400">Xml.each(path, match, ns = nil) do |node| … end</code></td>
                        <td class="py-3 px-4 text-gray-300">the number of matches yielded</td>
                    </tr>
                    <tr>
                        <td class="py-3 px-4 text-gray-300"><code class="text-amber-400">Xml.reader(path, match, ns = nil)</code></td>
                        <td class="py-3 px-4 text-gray-300">an <code class="text-amber-400">XmlReader</code>: <code class="text-amber-400">next</code> (the next match, or <code class="text-amber-400">nil</code> at the end), <code class="text-amber-400">count</code> (matches returned so far), <code class="text-amber-400">close</code></td>
                    </tr>
                </tbody>
            </table>
        </div>
        <ul class="space-y-2 text-gray-300 mb-6 list-disc pl-6">
            <li><strong class="text-white">Files only.</strong> For a String already in memory, use <code class="text-amber-400">Xml.parse(s).find_all(…)</code>. The path goes through the same checks as <code class="text-amber-400">Xml.parse_file</code> (the application-root jail under <code class="text-amber-400">soli serve</code> and in jobs, no symbolic links, no FIFOs or devices).</li>
            <li>**Each match is a detached <code class="text-amber-400">XmlNode</code>** carrying the namespace declarations in scope where it stood, so <code class="text-amber-400">find</code>, <code class="text-amber-400">xpath</code>, <code class="text-amber-400">to_h</code> and <code class="text-amber-400">to_xml</code> work on it alone. Its <code class="text-amber-400">parent</code> and <code class="text-amber-400">document</code> are <code class="text-amber-400">nil</code>.</li>
            <li><strong class="text-white">Only the outermost match is yielded.</strong> A match nested inside another is part of the outer one's subtree.</li>
            <li>**<code class="text-amber-400">break</code> inside an <code class="text-amber-400">Xml.each</code> block acts like <code class="text-amber-400">next</code>**: the loop goes on to the following match. This is the same for every block a built-in method runs (card #651). To stop early, use <code class="text-amber-400">Xml.reader</code>, where <code class="text-amber-400">break</code> in the <code class="text-amber-400">while</code> loop really stops. A <code class="text-amber-400">throw</code> inside the block propagates and closes the file.</li>
            <li>The request deadline is checked before each match.</li>
            <li>An error in the file raises when the reader reaches it, with a line and column, even after the last match: <code class="text-amber-400">Xml.each: line 3, column 9: expected &lt;/item&gt;, found &lt;/r&gt;</code>. An <code class="text-amber-400">XmlReader</code> raises it once, then returns <code class="text-amber-400">nil</code>.</li>
        </ul>
        <p class="text-gray-300 mb-4">The match is a smaller language than a query, because the reader keeps no state beyond the chain of open elements:</p>
        <ul class="space-y-2 text-gray-300 mb-6 list-disc pl-6">
            <li>steps <code class="text-amber-400">name</code>, <code class="text-amber-400">p:name</code>, <code class="text-amber-400">*:name</code>, <code class="text-amber-400">*</code> and <code class="text-amber-400">p:*</code>, joined by <code class="text-amber-400">/</code> (<code class="text-amber-400">catalog/product</code> matches a <code class="text-amber-400">product</code> whose parent is a <code class="text-amber-400">catalog</code>, anywhere), with an optional leading <code class="text-amber-400">/</code> (from the root) or <code class="text-amber-400">//</code>;</li>
            <li>one predicate, on the last step only: <code class="text-amber-400">[@a]</code>, <code class="text-amber-400">[@a='v']</code> or <code class="text-amber-400">[@a!='v']</code>.</li>
        </ul>
        <p class="text-gray-300 mb-4">Anything else raises <code class="text-amber-400">Xml.each: unsupported match at column 9: only [@a], [@a='v'] and [@a!='v'] are supported while streaming</code>. Unprefixed names match in any namespace, as with <code class="text-amber-400">find</code>; <code class="text-amber-400">{"": uri}</code> in <code class="text-amber-400">ns</code> pins them to one, and prefixes resolve through <code class="text-amber-400">ns</code>, then the root's declarations.</p>
        <p class="text-gray-300 mb-4"><code class="text-amber-400">SOLI_MAX_XML_BYTES</code> and <code class="text-amber-400">SOLI_MAX_XML_NODES</code> cap <strong class="text-white">one match</strong>, and one text run or attribute value (checked before it is buffered). <code class="text-amber-400">SOLI_MAX_XML_DEPTH</code> applies to the whole file. The file itself has no size cap.</p>
    </section>

    <section id="errors" class="scroll-mt-20 mb-12">
        <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-white mb-4">Errors</h2>
        <p class="text-gray-300 mb-4">Errors are ordinary runtime errors, Strings with a stable prefix (<code class="text-amber-400">Xml.parse:</code>, <code class="text-amber-400">Xml.parse_file:</code>, <code class="text-amber-400">Xml.find:</code>, <code class="text-amber-400">Xml.xpath:</code>, <code class="text-amber-400">Xml.each:</code>, <code class="text-amber-400">Xml.reader:</code>, <code class="text-amber-400">Xml.element:</code>, <code class="text-amber-400">XmlNode.add:</code>…) and, for input errors, a line and column:</p>
        <pre data-filename="Example" class="mb-6"><code class="language-soli text-sm">broken = "&lt;a&gt;&lt;b&gt;&lt;/a&gt;"
message = nil
try
  Xml.parse(broken)
catch error
  message = error
end
# message: "Xml.parse: line 1, column 7: expected &lt;/b&gt;, found &lt;/a&gt;"

fallback = Xml.parse("&lt;oops") rescue nil   # =&gt; nil</code></pre>
        <p class="text-gray-300 mb-4">Some messages:</p>
        <ul class="space-y-2 text-gray-300 mb-6 list-disc pl-6">
            <li><code class="text-amber-400">Xml.parse: line 1, column 1: DOCTYPE declarations are not allowed in XML documents</code></li>
            <li><code class="text-amber-400">Xml.parse: line 1, column 4: unknown entity '&amp;nbsp;'</code></li>
            <li><code class="text-amber-400">Xml.parse: line 1, column 5: second root element &lt;b&gt;</code></li>
            <li><code class="text-amber-400">Xml.parse: line 1, column 7: more than 2 nested elements (SOLI_MAX_XML_DEPTH)</code></li>
            <li><code class="text-amber-400">Xml.xpath: unbound prefix 'q'</code></li>
            <li><code class="text-amber-400">XmlNode.append: would create a cycle</code></li>
        </ul>
        <p class="text-gray-300 mb-4"><code class="text-amber-400">Xml.well_formed?</code> answers without raising.</p>
    </section>

    <section id="equality" class="scroll-mt-20 mb-12">
        <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-white mb-4">Equality</h2>
        <p class="text-gray-300 mb-4"><code class="text-amber-400">==</code> is identity: two handles are equal when they point at the same node of the same tree. So <code class="text-amber-400">a.parent == b.parent</code>, <code class="text-amber-400">includes?</code> and <code class="text-amber-400">uniq</code> work on nodes. It is not structural: <code class="text-amber-400">Xml.parse(s) == Xml.parse(s)</code> is <code class="text-amber-400">false</code>; compare <code class="text-amber-400">to_xml</code> instead. A node is always truthy.</p>
    </section>

    <section id="limits" class="scroll-mt-20 mb-12">
        <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-white mb-4">Limits</h2>
        <p class="text-gray-300 mb-4">Every limit can be changed with an environment variable. Parse options can only lower them.</p>
        <div class="overflow-x-auto mb-6 rounded-xl border border-white/10">
            <table class="w-full text-sm">
                <thead>
                    <tr class="border-b border-white/10">
                        <th class="text-left py-3 px-4 text-gray-400 font-semibold">Variable</th>
                        <th class="text-left py-3 px-4 text-gray-400 font-semibold">Default</th>
                        <th class="text-left py-3 px-4 text-gray-400 font-semibold">Caps</th>
                    </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody class="divide-y divide-white/5">
                    <tr>
                        <td class="py-3 px-4 text-gray-300"><code class="text-amber-400">SOLI_MAX_XML_BYTES</code></td>
                        <td class="py-3 px-4 text-gray-300"><code class="text-amber-400">67108864</code> (64 MiB)</td>
                        <td class="py-3 px-4 text-gray-300">the input of <code class="text-amber-400">Xml.parse</code> and <code class="text-amber-400">Xml.parse_file</code>, one streamed match, one streamed text run or attribute, and the output of <code class="text-amber-400">to_xml</code></td>
                    </tr>
                    <tr>
                        <td class="py-3 px-4 text-gray-300"><code class="text-amber-400">SOLI_MAX_XML_DEPTH</code></td>
                        <td class="py-3 px-4 text-gray-300"><code class="text-amber-400">256</code></td>
                        <td class="py-3 px-4 text-gray-300">element nesting (the parser never goes past 10,000 whatever the value)</td>
                    </tr>
                    <tr>
                        <td class="py-3 px-4 text-gray-300"><code class="text-amber-400">SOLI_MAX_XML_NODES</code></td>
                        <td class="py-3 px-4 text-gray-300"><code class="text-amber-400">1000000</code></td>
                        <td class="py-3 px-4 text-gray-300">nodes in one tree, removed and built nodes included</td>
                    </tr>
                    <tr>
                        <td class="py-3 px-4 text-gray-300"><code class="text-amber-400">SOLI_MAX_XML_ATTRS</code></td>
                        <td class="py-3 px-4 text-gray-300"><code class="text-amber-400">1024</code></td>
                        <td class="py-3 px-4 text-gray-300">attributes on one element</td>
                    </tr>
                    <tr>
                        <td class="py-3 px-4 text-gray-300"><code class="text-amber-400">SOLI_MAX_XPATH_VISITS</code></td>
                        <td class="py-3 px-4 text-gray-300"><code class="text-amber-400">10000000</code></td>
                        <td class="py-3 px-4 text-gray-300">node visits in one query</td>
                    </tr>
                </tbody>
            </table>
        </div>
        <p class="text-gray-300 mb-4">Fixed limits: a name is at most 4096 bytes; an element declares at most 256 namespaces, and at most 4096 are in scope at once; an XPath expression is at most 4096 bytes and 64 steps; <code class="text-amber-400">to_xml(indent:)</code> indents by at most 64 spaces; <code class="text-amber-400">to_h</code> nests at most 1024 levels.</p>
    </section>

    <section id="soli-check" class="scroll-mt-20 mb-12">
        <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-white mb-4">soli check</h2>
        <p class="text-gray-300 mb-4"><code class="text-amber-400">soli check</code> knows the four classes. Annotate with <code class="text-amber-400">XmlNode</code> and <code class="text-amber-400">XmlDocument</code>:</p>
        <pre data-filename="Example" class="mb-6"><code class="language-soli text-sm">def item_title(item: XmlNode) -&gt; String
  item.find("name").text
end

def skus(doc: XmlDocument) -&gt; Array
  doc.find_all("p").map { |product| product["sku"] }
end</code></pre>
        <ul class="space-y-2 text-gray-300 mb-6 list-disc pl-6">
            <li>A <strong class="text-white">literal</strong> query is compiled when the file is checked: an invalid or unsupported expression is a check error, and the checker knows whether the result is a node or a String (<code class="text-amber-400">find("item/@id")</code> is a String). A query built at run time is typed as unknown.</li>
            <li>Methods that may return <code class="text-amber-400">nil</code> (<code class="text-amber-400">find</code>, <code class="text-amber-400">parent</code>, <code class="text-amber-400">root</code>, <code class="text-amber-400">node["id"]</code>) are typed as <code class="text-amber-400">XmlNode</code> or <code class="text-amber-400">String</code>; the checker has no nil type, so test for <code class="text-amber-400">nil</code> yourself.</li>
            <li>Only <code class="text-amber-400">node.text = …</code> and <code class="text-amber-400">node.name = …</code> may be assigned; any other property write on a node is a check error.</li>
            <li>A node may be the condition of an <code class="text-amber-400">if</code>, <code class="text-amber-400">unless</code>, <code class="text-amber-400">while</code> or ternary, so <code class="text-amber-400">while (item = reader.next)</code> checks. This holds for every class-typed value, not only XML.</li>
            <li>Options, attribute Hashes, namespace tables and block parameters are typed as unknown.</li>
            <li>Avoid naming a variable after a built-in function: <code class="text-amber-400">lines = doc.xpath(…)</code> is read as a new value for the built-in <code class="text-amber-400">lines</code> and refused (<code class="text-amber-400">Type mismatch: expected (Any) -&gt; Any[], found XmlNode[]</code>). Call it <code class="text-amber-400">line_items</code>.</li>
        </ul>
    </section>

    <section id="security" class="scroll-mt-20 mb-12">
        <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-white mb-4">Security</h2>
        <ul class="space-y-2 text-gray-300 mb-6 list-disc pl-6">
            <li><strong class="text-white">No DTDs, no entity expansion.</strong> A <code class="text-amber-400">DOCTYPE</code> is always refused, so external entities (XXE) and entity-expansion bombs ("billion laughs") cannot happen.</li>
            <li><strong class="text-white">Bounded work.</strong> Depth, node count, input size, attributes per element, namespace declarations and XPath work are all capped (see <a href="#limits" class="text-cyan-400 hover:text-cyan-300 underline">Limits</a>), and every error names the limit it hit.</li>
            <li><strong class="text-white">No path from a String.</strong> <code class="text-amber-400">Xml.parse</code> never reads a file. <code class="text-amber-400">Xml.parse_file</code>, <code class="text-amber-400">Xml.each</code> and <code class="text-amber-400">Xml.reader</code> take a path and apply the same jail as the <code class="text-amber-400">File</code> functions: inside the application root under <code class="text-amber-400">soli serve</code> and in jobs, no symbolic links, no FIFOs or devices.</li>
            <li><strong class="text-white">Output is always well-formed.</strong> The builder validates names, escapes text and values, drops characters XML cannot carry, and splits <code class="text-amber-400">]]&gt;</code> in CDATA. Building XML by string interpolation is how injection happens; prefer the builder, or at least <code class="text-amber-400">Xml.escape</code>.</li>
            <li><strong class="text-white">Retention.</strong> A node keeps its whole document in memory. Keep <code class="text-amber-400">dup</code>s of the parts you need from a large document, not handles into it.</li>
            <li><strong class="text-white">UTF-8 only.</strong> A document in another encoding raises; convert it first.</li>
        </ul>
    </section>

    <section id="soap-and-xml-signatures" class="scroll-mt-20 mb-12">
        <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-white mb-4">SOAP and XML signatures</h2>
        <p class="text-gray-300 mb-4"><code class="text-amber-400">SOAP.parse</code> and <code class="text-amber-400">SOAP.call</code> read XML with this same parser, with their own limits (nesting depth 64, 1 MiB of text per element) and their own lenient rules, and return a Hash. See <a href="/docs/builtins/soap" class="text-cyan-400 hover:text-cyan-300 underline">SOAP</a> for the shape and for what changed when they moved to this parser.</p>
        <p class="text-gray-300 mb-4"><code class="text-amber-400">Xml.c14n_exclusive</code>, <code class="text-amber-400">Xml.get_element_by_id</code> and <code class="text-amber-400">Xml.get_elements_by_tag</code> (exclusive canonicalisation for XML-DSig and SAML) are documented under <a href="/docs/builtins/xml-signatures" class="text-cyan-400 hover:text-cyan-300 underline">XML Signatures</a>.</p>
    </section>
</div>
